# Shell scripts must keep LF endings regardless of the checkout platform: a CRLF shebang
# makes `./scripts/test.sh` fail with "bad interpreter" on Linux and macOS, and TEST_CMD in
# .claude/settings.json points at it, so that would take the Stop-hook gate down with it.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Consumed by ready-issues.sh / create-issues.sh with `read`, which leaves a trailing \r on
# the last field and makes an empty dependency list look non-empty.
*.tsv text eol=lf

# The corpora are content-addressed: every manifest declares a sha256 over the bytes of each
# member, and `python -m harness.corpora.build --check` regenerates and diffs them. A CRLF
# checkout on Windows would change every one of those hashes, so `fixtures/` is pinned to LF
# in the working tree on every platform.
fixtures/** text eol=lf

# The sample answer-sheet PDFs are byte-exact: their cross-reference tables hold byte offsets,
# so a CRLF checkout on Windows would damage every file. They contain no NUL byte, so Git
# would otherwise guess "text". (Same reason as the fixtures/ rule above.)
docs/live-tests/sample-materials/pdf/** binary

# The F-AUTHOR intake fixtures (#753) are generated containers: the AF-15 .docx embeds a PNG
# whose signature is `\x89PNG\r\n\x1a\n` -- the \r\n is mandated by the PNG spec, so the
# fixtures/** rule above strips it on commit and a fresh clone checks out bytes the
# generator cannot reproduce (the census diffs bytes). Git guesses "text" on the stored XML,
# so these need the explicit binary mark. (Same reason as the sample-PDF rule above.)
fixtures/F-AUTHOR/**/*.docx binary
fixtures/F-AUTHOR/**/*.doc binary

# The SPA rebuild (TC-UI-01 (c), `tests/browser/spa/test_spa_rebuild.py`) diffs a fresh
# `vite build` against the committed bundle **byte for byte**, and the build output inherits
# the line endings of the `ui/` sources it reads. With `core.autocrlf=true` a Windows checkout
# turned the sources (and the committed `.js`/`.css`/`.svg` assets) into CRLF, so the gate read
# a "stale bundle" whose only difference was the checkout's own conversion — and the bundle's
# `index.html` was committed from that CRLF working tree, so its stored bytes were
# platform-tainted and the gate failed on POSIX too. Both sides are pinned to LF here: the
# sources so every platform builds the same bytes, the committed bundle so a checkout cannot
# rewrite them under the gate. The `.woff2` fonts are binary data whose byte stream may carry
# `\r\n` sequences — they keep git's binary detection (`text eol=lf` on them would corrupt
# the font).
ui/** text eol=lf
ui/src/fonts/*.woff2 binary
src/aeh/console_assets/spa/index.html text eol=lf
src/aeh/console_assets/spa/assets/*.js text eol=lf
src/aeh/console_assets/spa/assets/*.css text eol=lf
src/aeh/console_assets/spa/assets/*.svg text eol=lf
